#speculation(title: [Histamine-Orexin Arousal Bridge and the H1-Blocker Sedation Threshold])[
Histaminergic and orexinergic wake systems interact directly: orexin neurons act on monoaminergic targets — including histaminergic tuberomammillary neurons — to maintain arousal @Sakurai2021OrexinMonoamine, and both systems are canonical wake-promoting neuromodulators @Jones2020ArousalCircuits. Wake-promoting neuromodulators (histamine, orexin, norepinephrine) are further tied to sleep quality and glymphatic clearance @Pedersen2026WakeNeuromodulators.

Applied to MCAS: episodic mast-cell histamine surges would transiently add to the central histaminergic arousal load, which could contribute to the "wired but tired" mismatch — high arousal alongside the fatigue and non-restorative sleep that impaired clearance is proposed to produce @Pedersen2026WakeNeuromodulators. By symmetry, the sedation threshold of an H1 blocker — the dose at which central H1 blockade first emerges — could index a patient's central histamine arousal load: a lower threshold would mark a smaller central histaminergic reserve, a higher one a larger compensatory drive. The orexin-histamine interaction is established in animals and in reviews of human circuitry; no study has measured a histamine arousal load or an H1-blocker sedation threshold in ME/CFS (translation gap). (Certainty: 0.35)

_Falsifiable prediction:_ A pharmacodynamic measure of the H1-blocker sedation threshold (dose at which objective vigilance first falls) correlates with a marker of central histaminergic or orexinergic tone (for example cerebrospinal-fluid orexin-A or histamine), and ME/CFS patients with a "wired but tired" profile show a distinct, higher threshold than those without it.

*Consequence:* The sedation threshold would become a bedside readout of central histamine arousal load, letting clinicians dose antihistamines against a named neurobiological variable rather than trial-and-error — and giving "wired but tired" a specific neuromodulator correlate.
